WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.393
Beavers once lived all over Europe and North America.

2
00:00:04.394 --> 00:00:09.485
But because they have beautiful fur, people hunted them to make warm coats.

3
00:00:09.486 --> 00:00:17.762
As beavers started to disappear, scientists became worried and decided to learn more about them.

4
00:00:17.763 --> 00:00:22.087
The first thing scientists studied was a beaver’s body.

5
00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:27.816
Beavers are part of the rodent family, which means squirrels and rats are their cousins.

6
00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:34.967
Their front teeth are very long, and beavers use them to cut wood and pull off bark.

7
00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:41.655
Like a hamster or rat, a beaver’s teeth grow and grow and grow for its whole life!

8
00:00:41.656 --> 00:00:45.491
However, beavers are bigger than all of their cousins.

9
00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:54.916
Most beavers weigh fifteen to thirty-one kilograms, and are almost 117 centimeters long!

10
00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:59.826
Even though beavers have fur, they like to be in water instead of on land.

11
00:00:59.827 --> 00:01:04.717
Beavers are very beautiful swimmers, but they look silly when they try to walk.

12
00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:09.108
Their heavy tails make walking very difficult.

13
00:01:09.109 --> 00:01:17.211
A beaver tail is the size of a computer keyboard, and it helps a beaver stand, balance heavy logs, and steer in the water.

14
00:01:17.212 --> 00:01:25.934
And, if an enemy comes too close, one beaver will warn other beavers by slapping its tail on the water.

15
00:01:25.935 --> 00:01:30.152
When a beaver dives into the water, two important changes happen.

16
00:01:30.153 --> 00:01:34.569
First, it closes its eyes, nose, and mouth.

17
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.036
Beavers have two kinds of eyelids:

18
00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:42.898
the inside one is clear—like goggles—and the outside one is black. When

19
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:51.391
a beaver is underwater, it protects its eyes by using the goggle-like eyelids to see where it’s swimming.

20
00:01:51.392 --> 00:01:57.624
The beaver’s nose also has special skin inside which closes to keep out water.

21
00:01:57.625 --> 00:02:00.891
However, its mouth is even more amazing.

22
00:02:00.892 --> 00:02:07.774
A beaver needs its front teeth to grab logs underwater, but it doesn’t want a mouthful of water and logs.

23
00:02:07.775 --> 00:02:17.380
Amazingly, its lips are behind its front teeth so that it can keep water out and still grab whatever it needs.

24
00:02:17.381 --> 00:02:22.509
The second underwater change is when the beaver goes from a walker to a swimmer.

25
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.743
On land, a beaver’s back feet look like its cousin's.

26
00:02:26.744 --> 00:02:32.206
But when it dives into water, the toes pull apart to show a web in between.

27
00:02:32.207 --> 00:02:36.192
This webbing helps it swim through water quickly.

28
00:02:36.193 --> 00:02:39.919
Most beavers stay underwater for three to five minutes.

29
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.712
But some can stay down for as long as fifteen minutes.

30
00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:53.633
That’s a long time for only one breath—especially since most humans stay underwater for less than sixty seconds.

31
00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:57.179
Scientists were very impressed with the beaver’s body.

32
00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.441
Few animals can live in water and on land as well as the beaver.

33
00:03:01.442 --> 00:03:04.274
But scientists are still worried.

34
00:03:04.275 --> 00:03:09.825
The more that people wear fur coats, the fewer beavers there are in the wild.

35
00:03:09.826 --> 00:03:15.410
And the fewer beavers there are in the wild, the more problems scientists see in nature.

36
00:03:15.411 --> 00:03:21.030
Now that scientists have learned about a beaver’s body, they know more about how a beaver lives.

37
00:03:21.031 --> 00:03:27.087
But scientists are still looking for ways to help beavers and humans live together.

